# PageHeader — the bar at the top of a routed column that tells you which page you
# are on: a leading affordance, a title, a subtitle.
#
# IT USED TO CARRY NO BACK LINK, and could not: a back affordance needs a
# destination, and `link "…" -> "{href}"` was rejected before it was ever matched
# against a route, because the compiler validated the SHAPE of a link path and a
# fully interpolated path has no shape. Route expressions fixed that — but the
# back link still is not a parameter here, because a header's leading affordance
# is not always a link: it is a back arrow on a detail page, a close button in a
# flow, an avatar on a profile, and nothing at all on a top-level screen.
#
# So it is a `slot`, and the caller writes what it needs:
#
#     use PageHeader("Post", "12 replies"):
#         use Link("← Back", backHref)
#
# STICKINESS MOVED OUT OF THE HEADER AND INTO ITS OWN COMPONENT. `.x-pagehead` was
# `position: sticky; top: 0` — written for the three-column app shell, where the
# nav never scrolls and the centre column does, and losing the name of the thing
# you are reading three screens down is the failure it closed. That is a property
# of the CONTAINER, not of the header, and a site that already has a sticky top
# bar got two of them: the page header pinned itself under a nav that was pinned
# at the same `top: 0`, so they overlapped instead of stacking. It was reported,
# and the call site had to write `position: static` back over an imported rule.
#
# A component cannot ask what it is inside, so the caller says which it wants:
#
#     use PageHeader("Settings", "")           # in a page that scrolls as a whole
#     use StickyPageHeader("Home", "")         # in an app column that scrolls alone
#
# `StickyPageHeader` is the same header inside a sticky wrapper — the wrapper
# forwards its own children into the header's slot, so the leading affordance still
# works — and it pins at `var(--x-pagehead-top, 0px)`. THAT CUSTOM PROPERTY IS THE
# OTHER HALF OF THE FIX: two sticky bars in one scroll container are not a mistake,
# they are a nav above a header, and they stack correctly the moment the lower one
# knows the height of the upper one. Set `--x-pagehead-top: 56px` on any ancestor
# and the header parks under the bar instead of behind it, with no override of an
# imported rule.
app FacetLayout:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-pagehead { padding: 12px 16px; display: flex;
                    flex-direction: column; gap: 2px;
                    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--x-line, #2f3336);
                    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fa-bg) 85%, transparent);
                    backdrop-filter: blur(12px); }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-pagehead-title { font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.2; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-pagehead-sub { color: var(--fa-muted); font-size: 13px; }
        # The leading affordance sits above the title, at the size of the subtitle,
        # so it reads as chrome rather than as a second heading.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-pagehead > .fa-use { font-size: 13px; }
        # The sticky wrapper. `position: sticky` has to be on an element that is a
        # child of the scroll container, so it is the wrapper itself and not the
        # header inside it — and the wrapper is exactly the header's box, because it
        # has no padding of its own.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-pagehead-sticky { position: sticky; top: var(--x-pagehead-top, 0px);
                    z-index: 20; }
    component PageHeader(title: text, subtitle: text):
        box class "x-pagehead":
            slot
            text "{title}" class "x-pagehead-title"
            if subtitle != "":
                text "{subtitle}" class "x-pagehead-sub"
    component StickyPageHeader(title: text, subtitle: text):
        box class "x-pagehead-sticky":
            use PageHeader(title, subtitle):
                slot
